import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BN3Eol-0.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"UXS-014 — /explore v2 · navigating the known universe","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"uxs/UXS-014.md","filePath":"uxs/UXS-014.md"}'),n={name:"uxs/UXS-014.md"};function r(i,e,l,h,d,c){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="uxs-014-—-explore-v2-·-navigating-the-known-universe" tabindex="-1">UXS-014 — /explore v2 · navigating the known universe <a class="header-anchor" href="#uxs-014-—-explore-v2-·-navigating-the-known-universe" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;UXS-014 — /explore v2 · navigating the known universe&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Status · Draft v0.1 Visual fidelity · Spec-only at kickoff; signature moments (boundary crossing, warp, lens toggle) get a prototype / visual anchor gating Slice 0 (per the &quot;visual anchor before UX commit&quot; rule) IA anchor · §explore (extends the existing solar-system route outward) Related PRD · PRD-030 · Related RFC · RFC-032</p></blockquote><h2 id="why-this-uxs-exists" tabindex="-1">Why this UXS exists <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-uxs-exists" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why this UXS exists&quot;">​</a></h2><p>PRD-030 sets the <em>what/why</em> and RFC-032 the <em>how it&#39;s built</em>; this UXS owns the part that makes or breaks the epic — <strong>how it feels to move through the universe</strong>. The navigation is novel (zoom <em>and</em> warp, nested contexts, physics lenses), and without a pinned interaction model it gets designed ad-hoc in scene code — the chaos we&#39;re explicitly avoiding. This spec is the contract for movement, orientation, selection, transitions, and discovery, on desktop and mobile.</p><h2 id="the-one-rule-you-always-know-where-you-are" tabindex="-1">The one rule: you always know where you are <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-one-rule-you-always-know-where-you-are" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The one rule: you always know where you are&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With teleporting instead of continuous travel, orientation is the scarcest resource. Every 3D scale carries the <strong>level picker / &quot;you are here&quot;</strong>: one chip (the level&#39;s glyph, its name, a caret) that opens the ladder of all eight levels, always visible, each rung a warp to that level. Under it hangs the <strong>level card</strong>, &quot;what you are looking at&quot;: what the level is, its story, and up to five <code>/science</code> links (<a href="./decisions/UXD-027-explore-levels-one-table-glyphs-card-and-hint">UXD-027</a>). The breadcrumb this section first described (<code>Solar System › Orion Spur › Milky Way › Local Group</code>) was retired in #45; the picker replaced it. This is non-negotiable — it&#39;s what keeps discovery from becoming disorientation.</p><h2 id="navigation-model" tabindex="-1">Navigation model <a class="header-anchor" href="#navigation-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Navigation model&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Zoom within a context</strong> — the existing /explore camera controls, unchanged. Scroll/pinch out keeps pulling back.</li><li><strong>The boundary crossing (the signature moment)</strong> — as you zoom out past the Kuiper/Oort, the solar system smoothly <strong>collapses to a single luminous dot</strong> and the real nearby-star field <strong>fades in around it</strong>, re-based into the Neighborhood context. Continuous, legible, cinematic — no hard cut. This is the emotional core of v2; it gets a prototype before code.</li><li><strong>Warp between contexts</strong> — beyond the Neighborhood, you don&#39;t crawl through empty black. Click a pin (a galaxy, the galactic center) → a <strong>1–2 s cinematic warp</strong> with a caption that <em>teaches the distance</em> (&quot;warping 4.24 light-years to Proxima Centauri…&quot;, &quot;2.5 million years to Andromeda…&quot;). The warp motivates from a real click and always lands you oriented (the level picker updates, target framed).</li><li><strong>Two doors in</strong> — (a) <strong>wander</strong> and stumble on objects in the dark (the discovery joy), and (b) <strong>jump</strong> straight from a deep-sky gallery photo or an object index via <code>?goto=&lt;id&gt;</code> (browse-as-collection). Same objects, two ways.</li></ul><h2 id="the-level-picker-the-level-card-and-the-welcome-hint" tabindex="-1">The level picker, the level card and the welcome hint <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-level-picker-the-level-card-and-the-welcome-hint" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The level picker, the level card and the welcome hint&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Decided in <a href="./decisions/UXD-027-explore-levels-one-table-glyphs-card-and-hint">UXD-027</a>; looks in <a href="./../concept/05_Design_System">Design System §5.15 and §5.17</a>.</p><ul><li><strong>One table.</strong> The eight levels (name, glyph, <code>?context=</code> query, description, story, science links) are <code>SCALE_SHELLS</code> (<code>src/lib/explore/scale-shells.ts</code>). The nav&#39;s Explore dropdown (kept: D4), the hub, the home grid, the level picker, the level card and the Science Lens story all read it.</li><li><strong>The picker shows what a level is.</strong> The chip and every rung lead with the level&#39;s glyph, the one the home grid draws; with a pointer a rung adds the home-grid line under its name. The Explore rows of the nav carry the same glyph.</li><li><strong>The level card.</strong> Under the picker&#39;s row, at the right edge, 280 px, left of a docked panel: the level&#39;s glyph and name, what it is, its story, up to five <code>/science</code> links (titles in the page language). It follows the level; hidden in 2D and in a takeover (black hole, deep-sky, body scene). Open with a pointer in a roomy window, folded on touch and in a small window, the choice remembered. The Science Lens panel keeps its layer toggles; the story is the card&#39;s, so a level with no layers has no lens panel.</li><li><strong>The welcome hint.</strong> One line inviting a zoom, on <code>/explore</code> and on <code>/earth /moon /mars /venus</code>: gone at the first wheel turn, pinch, tap or drag on the canvas, at its ×, or after 12 s; not remembered. One sentence, not a tutorial.</li></ul><h2 id="selecting-understanding-an-object" tabindex="-1">Selecting &amp; understanding an object <a class="header-anchor" href="#selecting-understanding-an-object" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Selecting &amp; understanding an object&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Star, deep-sky, Milky Way, Local Group, Local Sheet, Virgo, Laniakea, Cosmic Web and black-hole panels share one body (<code>UniversePanelBody</code>, <a href="./decisions/UXD-023-star-and-outer-panels-follow-the-planet-pattern">UXD-023</a>): one hero or none, tabs on <code>PanelTabRow</code>, a star&#39;s constellation finder in its IN THE SKY tab. A black hole is picked at its own scale and opens its panel in place; the lensing render is the panel&#39;s action. The heroes of the outer-scale and black-hole panels come from <code>universe/panel-heroes.json</code> (gallery photos through the deep-sky loader, a photo of a part captioned &quot;Pictured: …&quot;; Triangulum shows NGC 604 since phase 4 of the galaxy pages), and the entity pages (<code>/explore/deep-sky|galaxies|structures/&lt;id&gt;</code>) show the same picture as one figure under the lede (UXS-001, ADR-096 items 17 and 18). The star pages do too, for the seven stars whose photo is a photograph of the star, cropped as their panel (ADR-096 item 13).</p><p>Click any star / planet / deep-sky object → the existing <strong>Panel</strong> opens (bottom-sheet mobile / right drawer desktop), with tactical detail from real data: name, distance (&quot;light left in…&quot;), type/spectral class, and — for exoplanet systems — the full per-planet treatment mirroring our solar-system planets. A deep-sky object&#39;s panel leads with its reviewed one-line <code>fact</code> and a <code>bio</code> paragraph in the reader&#39;s language (en-US fallback), then the catalogue values and their sources; only objects that open a panel show it (a photo-backed object or one with a culture door). Selection styling + hover mirror the current /explore conventions. Everything localized (i18n) like /science; every fact carries provenance.</p><p><strong>Select = focus</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-019-select-equals-focus">UXD-019</a>). Selecting an object, by canvas click, INDEX row, link or <code>?id=</code>, does three things: opens its Panel, rings it (a belt brightens instead; × closes the panel and the ring stays until another selection, Reset View or the chip clears it), and moves the camera onto it (a planet or moon at 8 radii, a small body at 14, a belt framed whole from the Sun; at the outer scales the camera pans to the member and eases in to 0.6 x the entry distance, and the scale&#39;s Reset gives it back). With a side drawer open the object sits in the middle of the space the drawer leaves. A chip turned off clears a selection of its kind; selecting an object whose chip is off turns the chip on. An id with no object in the scene is plain text, never a dead link. A mission linked from a body&#39;s panel opens in place, newest first.</p><p><strong>Hover and selection feedback</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-020-explore-readability-pins-rings-labels">UXD-020</a>): every pin at every scale (and the planet or small body under the cursor in the solar system) shows one thin ring on hover and selection, 44 px and screen-constant at the scales with pins, in the state blue <code>#889fe7</code>; the &quot;you are here&quot; anchor keeps a gold ring up at the seven levels that have one (not the solar system). A selected pin keeps its ring and name while another is hovered. Names are 12 px at every scale, hidden when they would overlap a more important one (the hovered and selected name is always shown), and a name does not move when its pin is hovered.</p><p><strong>The HUD numbers and names</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-021-explore-hud-scale-bar-picker-pills-references">UXD-021</a>): the scale bar at the lower right keeps one width (120 px, 64 px on a phone) at every zoom and its value follows the zoom, to three significant digits (a round number at a moving width is gone); the box around it keeps one size (the label sits under the bar) and, with a side panel open, sits left of the panel. The solar system and the neighbourhood draw a bar; the six schematic scales do not (they are not to scale). The scale picker&#39;s ladder opens leftward when the row is flush right and stays on screen; with a side panel open the row sits left of the panel. There is one RESET VIEW per scale, always drawn, disabled until there is something to reset: the HUD row&#39;s at the solar system, the navigator&#39;s at the other seven. The &quot;← Vega&quot; and &quot;→ star&quot; pills show only while ʻOumuamua or the craft is hovered or selected. The REFERENCES overlay draws the eight planets at true relative size and lists their names and diameters in an aligned grid, in the reader&#39;s language.</p><p><a id="the-orbit-ruler"></a><strong>The orbit ruler</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-024-orbit-ruler-one-row-draws-its-regime">UXD-024</a>; look in Design System §5.16): the lower-left reference of /explore, /earth, /moon and /mars. Every row is one line (the ruler grows from 208 to 280 px to its longest row; a name that cannot fit is cut with an ellipsis and carries the full name as its title). Hovering a row or focusing it with the keyboard draws that regime in the scene in the highlight blue (a band, a line, a shell, or a pin ring on a Lagrange point&#39;s residents; beyond the scene on /explore, dashed), a click or Enter opens the regime panel and the regime stays drawn while it is open. The regime colour stays on the row&#39;s dot. The header folds the ruler (<code>▾</code> / <code>▸</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>); the choice is remembered and a window under 560 px high starts folded. On <code>/explore</code> the ruler is drawn folded while the Iconic missions legend is open (operator, 2026-11-13; the saved choice is untouched and comes back when the legend closes; UXD-026 decision 10). On the Worlds the locate chip, the &quot;AS SEEN FROM YOU&quot; readout and the ruler are one column in the lower-left corner; on a phone the chip is the bar&#39;s first icon and the ruler is the drawer&#39;s Ruler tab. In the regime panel a resident with an image shows it as a 28 px photo ringed in its agency colour, one without keeps the dot; the photo is the thumb of the entity&#39;s blessed hero slot (<a href="./decisions/UXD-025-lists-carry-a-round-photo">UXD-025</a>). 23 of the 38 founding missions are links (on /explore they open in place), each with the mission&#39;s photo before the year, ringed in its agency colour (the other 15 keep the dot), and none ends in a country tag.</p><p><strong>The iconic missions legend and its boxes</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-026-iconic-missions-legend-and-milestone-boxes">UXD-026</a>; look in Design System §5.14 and §5.15): ICONIC MISSIONS is the first layer chip and opens the legend under the HUD row. Its first line is the title, &quot;Hover any spacecraft to read why it&#39;s iconic&quot;, with an (i) that opens the why-one-plane popover (title, two sentences, a &quot;Read full&quot; link to <code>/science/transfers/coplanar-trajectories</code>); then 18 rows of 28 px (36 px on touch) that lead with the mission&#39;s 28 px round photo ringed in its path colour (Voyager 2 magenta, <a href="./decisions/UXD-037-orbit-band-palette-no-teal">UXD-037</a>; <a href="./decisions/UXD-025-lists-carry-a-round-photo">UXD-025</a> T3b), the list scrolling alone and the legend ending above the lower-left controls (folded ruler, PLANET SCALES, time dock). A hovered or keyboard-focused row, or the craft&#39;s path under the pointer, shows its tagline in a card beside the legend, level with the row, so nothing in the list moves; a selection opens the mission panel. In the scene the highlighted mission shows a <strong>milestone box</strong> at each labelled waypoint (header <code>n/N · date</code>, the milestone name below, 12 px at every zoom, no mission name) and one <strong>mission box</strong> at Today (the name over where the craft is now). Boxes go through the shared label placement pass (§5.14): a box that would overlap another is hidden and returns on zoom, and the marker under the pointer shows its own. A click on a box or marker opens the mission (UXD-019).</p><p><strong>The orbit ruler&#39;s science cards</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-010-ruler-regimes-each-get-their-own-science-card">UXD-010</a>, <a href="./decisions/UXD-022-science-link-lists">UXD-022</a>): each regime pane of a ruler (Explore, Earth, Mars, Moon) carries one <code>/science</code> card, its own best match for what the pane teaches, and no card repeats inside one ruler (GIANTS shows the giants&#39; moon systems, BELT the asteroid belt, KUIPER the Kuiper belt, SCATTERED eccentricity, HELIOPAUSE the AU). Across the four rulers a concept may repeat where the physics is the same. The pair is the same in every locale.</p><p><strong>The way to the world pages</strong> (E6, D5, <a href="./decisions/UXD-028-explore-and-worlds-link-both-ways">UXD-028</a>): zooming into a planet never changes the page, because the two views have different cameras, data and state. The planet panels of Earth, Mars and Venus and the satellite panel of the Moon carry a &quot;Go to Mars&quot; (and so on) chip, the view chip of the action row, to <code>/earth /mars /venus /moon</code>, and each world page&#39;s view row starts with &quot;Back to Explore&quot;, a link to <code>/explore?id=&lt;body&gt;</code> (the Moon: <code>earth:moon</code>) that opens the body&#39;s panel with the camera on it. The body&#39;s page <code>/explore/&lt;id&gt;</code> carries the same link. One table, <code>src/lib/explore/world-links.ts</code>, feeds all three.</p><h2 id="the-index-on-every-scale" tabindex="-1">The INDEX on every scale <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-index-on-every-scale" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The INDEX on every scale&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every one of the eight scales (solar system, neighbourhood, Milky Way, Local Group, Local Sheet, Virgo, Laniakea, Cosmic web) has the same INDEX (<a href="./decisions/UXD-017-one-index-on-every-scale">UXD-017</a>; look and parts in Design System §5.13): a left-edge handle labelled INDEX opens a 320 px rail with a search box, kind chips, a count and a list of every selectable object of that scene, whether or not it has a page. A row selects the object as a canvas click does, and takes the camera (<a href="./decisions/UXD-019-select-equals-focus">UXD-019</a>); a <code>↗</code> link beside the row opens the object&#39;s page where one exists. The INDEX stays open after a select.</p><ul><li><strong>One state.</strong> One open flag for all eight scales. On a scale jump it stays open on a fine pointer and closes on a coarse one, where the rail covers most of the width.</li><li><strong>Where it is not offered.</strong> During a takeover (the black-hole lensing view, deep-sky immersion, an exoplanet&#39;s BodyScene), in the 2D view outside the solar system, and in Exhibit Mode.</li><li><strong>Chips per scale.</strong> After All, kind chips (the solar system: planets, dwarfs, asteroids with Arrokoth, moons, comets with ʻOumuamua, so every row but the Sun and the two belts is under a chip; the neighbourhood: planetary systems, culture doors, deep sky; the outer scales: the scale&#39;s kinds), plus Notable objects where the data has headliners; at most five besides All.</li><li><strong>Solar-system rows are two lines</strong> (C6, UXD-017 amendment): the name over a meta line of distance, size and listed moons (<code>1.52 AU · 6,779 km · 2 major moons</code>; a dwarf, asteroid or Kuiper object <code>2.77 AU · 939 km</code>; a comet its distance; a moon <code>Jupiter · 3,643 km</code>; a belt <code>2.2–3.2 AU</code>; the Sun &quot;Star&quot;), in the reader&#39;s number and unit forms. The other seven scales keep one line.</li><li><strong>A round photo leads the solar-system rows</strong> (L45-T2, <a href="./decisions/UXD-025-lists-carry-a-round-photo">UXD-025</a>): the Sun, every planet, every moon, both belts and 12 of 23 small bodies show the 28 px <code>EntityThumb</code> of their blessed hero image in the lead slot, ring the body&#39;s colour (the planets, the Sun) or neutral; the 11 small bodies with no image keep the dot. On the other seven scales a row has a 28 px photo in a slot of its own before the name, the badges behind it (L46, UXD-025 T5): a star&#39;s DSS crop, a deep-sky object&#39;s photo, an outer row&#39;s panel photo or its twin&#39;s; a stand-in for a part (&quot;Pictured: NGC 253&quot;) says so on hover; a row with none shows the centred dot, so names line up. A closed INDEX loads no image.</li><li><strong>The kind of a row is a glyph</strong> (<a href="./decisions/UXD-045-index-row-type-is-a-glyph-with-a-tooltip">UXD-045</a>): on every scale but the solar system and the Local Group a row ends with a 14 px glyph of its type (a globular cluster, a galaxy, a nebula, a group, a void, a black hole ...) instead of the words &quot;Globular cluster&quot;, so the name keeps the row&#39;s width. The exact type is the tooltip (hover on the glyph, or keyboard focus on the row) and is read after the name by a screen reader. A star keeps its constellation as text before its glyph.</li><li><strong>Black holes</strong> sit in the scale their distance puts them in: Sagittarius A* and Cygnus X-1 in the Milky Way, M87* in Virgo, Gargantua (fiction, dashed and muted) in a Fiction section at the foot of the Cosmic web. Selecting one closes the INDEX and opens the lensing view.</li><li><strong>Prerender.</strong> <code>/explore</code> is one HTML file for every <code>?context=</code>, so all eight lists are in it, hidden unless on screen. Only rows with a page are written there, as links; the other rows join on hydration.</li><li><strong>Deep-sky objects.</strong> Every object of the neighbourhood&#39;s deep-sky section has a row, and a row is named as the object&#39;s page and panel name it: designation, then the common name (<code>M13 · Hercules Globular Cluster</code>, <code>M3 · NGC 5272</code>). Selecting one, by row, canvas click or <code>?deepsky=&lt;id&gt;</code>, opens its panel, rings it and turns the camera to it (select = focus); an object with a photo also warps in and immerses, one without opens the flat panel with no hero. The 120 objects with reviewed text have a page, <code>/explore/deep-sky/&lt;id&gt;</code> (ADR-096 item 17), and its <code>↗</code>. One real object has one page: the Milky Way globular rows (M3, M4, M5, M13, M15, M22, M92) and the Local Group rows of M32, M110, Andromeda and Triangulum link the deep-sky page of the same object, and take its name; the Local Group rows therefore appear in the prerendered HTML too. <strong>Galaxies and structures</strong> (ADR-096 item 18): the Local Group, Milky Way (Magellanic Clouds) and four structure scales link <code>/explore/galaxies/&lt;id&gt;</code> and <code>/explore/structures/&lt;id&gt;</code> through the same resolver (deep-sky first), where a page has its text: the en-US overlay files under <code>i18n-src/en-US/universe/galaxies</code> and <code>structures</code> decide, and today 29 galaxy and 30 structure pages exist (batches 05 and 06). A member with a page is a link row in the prerendered HTML and carries the <code>↗</code> once hydrated; a member without one stays a button.</li><li><strong>Folded filters</strong> (C6-F, UXD-017 amendment): only the chips that fit one line beside All are drawn; the others are behind a FILTERS button beside the count, which opens them in place and, closed, shows how many are on (the solar system: All, Planets, Dwarfs and Moons visible, Asteroids and Comets folded; the other scales fold the chips past the first line the same way).</li><li><strong>Z-order.</strong> The handle is at 44 (25 in a window 600 px wide or less with a mouse, so it sits under the full-width detail panel instead of over its text) and the rail at 45; the scale navigator rises to 46 while its ladder is open (Design System §4.7).</li><li>The Worlds (<code>/moon</code>, <code>/mars</code>, <code>/venus</code>, <code>/earth</code>) keep their own <code>SurfaceIndexPanel</code>; a site&#39;s photo (L45-T2: the thumb of the image its panel shows, <a href="./decisions/UXD-025-lists-carry-a-round-photo">UXD-025</a>) is ringed in its nation colour, the nation legend&#39;s, the dot of a site with no photo is that colour, and the row&#39;s meta line ends with <code>In orbit</code> or <code>On land</code> (an Earth object keeps its own colour).</li></ul><h2 id="lenses-physics-overlays" tabindex="-1">Lenses (physics overlays) <a class="header-anchor" href="#lenses-physics-overlays" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lenses (physics overlays)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Reuse the <strong>Science-Lens</strong> control already on /explore. Lenses are toggles on the current scene, not separate screens: <strong>curvature</strong> (spacetime grid warps around mass — labeled as an analogy), <strong>time</strong> (dilation readout + light-travel-time), <strong>causality</strong> (light-cone spheres), <strong>property-space</strong> (re-projects the same star field onto physical axes — the flyable Hertzsprung-Russell diagram). Only offer lenses meaningful in the active context. A lens never hides the level picker or the exit.</p><h2 id="culture-doors" tabindex="-1">Culture doors <a class="header-anchor" href="#culture-doors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Culture doors&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Optional overlay (off by default) that attaches a story card to a real object (Vega → <em>Contact</em>, Gargantua → <em>Interstellar</em>, Kepler-16b → Tatooine). Clearly badged <strong>fiction over fact</strong> — the real object&#39;s science stays primary; the story is a labeled layer. Entry point for later messaging/discussion.</p><h2 id="responsive-performance-desktop-mobile" tabindex="-1">Responsive &amp; performance (desktop + mobile) <a class="header-anchor" href="#responsive-performance-desktop-mobile" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Responsive &amp; performance (desktop + mobile)&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The <strong>device budget</strong> (RFC-032 C-C) governs dot density: the journey is identical on phone and desktop, the <em>count</em> adapts. Core navigation (zoom, warp, select, the level picker) is full parity everywhere.</li><li>Heaviest lenses/shaders (gravitational lensing) may be <strong>desktop-first with a graceful mobile tier</strong> — never a hard &quot;unsupported,&quot; always a legible fallback.</li><li>Touch gestures: one finger orbits (a flat 2D map pans), two fingers pinch to zoom AND drag to pan, together, as on every 3D scene (<code>touch-gestures.ts</code>; the pan is the shift/right-drag pan of the mouse); tap-a-pin for warp; the level picker row is a tappable 44px target row.</li></ul><h2 id="accessibility" tabindex="-1">Accessibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#accessibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accessibility&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Extends the existing /explore a11y (RFC-031): the object index is keyboard-reachable (the DOM &quot;mirror&quot; pattern — every selectable object in a list, <code>?goto=</code> works without the canvas; one INDEX per scale, one Tab stop, arrow keys, Enter selects, see &quot;The INDEX on every scale&quot;), the level picker is keyboard-navigable (arrow keys between rungs, Escape closes), the level card&#39;s fold is a labelled disclosure button, the welcome hint is a polite status region, warps announce their destination to screen readers, reduced-motion shortens/skips the warp cinematics and the boundary-collapse animation.</p><h2 id="signature-moments-that-get-a-visual-anchor-before-build" tabindex="-1">Signature moments that get a visual anchor before build <a class="header-anchor" href="#signature-moments-that-get-a-visual-anchor-before-build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Signature moments that get a visual anchor before build&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Boundary crossing</strong> — Sun → one dot among real stars.</li><li><strong>A warp</strong> — the cinematic hop + distance caption + re-orientation.</li><li><strong>A lens toggle</strong> — the map re-rendered through physics (curvature grid).</li></ol><p>These three are prototyped/mocked on real screenshots and gated on Marko&#39;s approval before the corresponding slice ships.</p>',35)])])}const g=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
